{
  "summary": "This study addresses the challenge of achieving stable stoichiometric pure water splitting over Ta3N5 by identifying incompatibility between cocatalysts in alkaline water oxidation and pure water splitting as a critical bottleneck. Using a multi-step incipient wetness impregnation strategy, researchers developed a highly dispersed RuCoOx cocatalyst. The optimized Pt@Cr2O3/Ta3N5/RuCoOx system achieved 0.72% apparent quantum yield at 420 nm for pure water splitting, overcoming photocorrosion-induced N2 generation through enhanced hole extraction and reduced reaction barriers at the interface.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Identification of cocatalyst incompatibility between alkaline water oxidation and pure water splitting as a bottleneck causing photocorrosion and N2 generation",
    "Development of a highly dispersed RuCoOx cocatalyst via multi-step incipient wetness impregnation",
    "Achievement of 0.72% apparent quantum yield at 420 nm for stoichiometric pure water splitting with Pt@Cr2O3/Ta3N5/RuCoOx"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes cocatalyst compatibility as a fundamental design principle for stable photocatalytic pure water splitting, providing mechanistic insights for advancing nitride and oxynitride photocatalysts."
}

{
  "summary": "This review reassesses the pro-tumor role of M2-like tumor-associated macrophages (TAMs) by evaluating functional evidence from in vitro and tumor-derived studies. It examines phagocytosis, antigen processing, cross-presentation, and CD8+ T cell activation. Findings indicate that some CD206+ TAMs can cross-present tumor antigens and activate CD8+ T cells comparably to dendritic cells, correlating with improved outcomes. Conversely, other macrophages may promote immunosuppression. The study emphasizes that antigen fate and co-stimulatory context, not M1/M2 markers, determine functional outcomes.",
  "key_findings": [
    "CD206+ tumor-associated macrophages can cross-present tumor antigens and activate CD8+ T cells at levels comparable to conventional dendritic cells",
    "These macrophages correlate with better clinical outcomes in specific contexts",
    "Antigen-presenting macrophages may either activate cytotoxic T cells or promote regulatory T cell activity depending on co-stimulatory context"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This review establishes that functional capacity, not M1/M2 marker expression, determines the immunological role of tumor-associated macrophages in cancer, shifting the focus from static markers to dynamic antigen-presenting mechanisms."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the factors influencing early human settlement in desert regions, focusing on the role of lithic raw materials beyond water availability. Using a 1,500-km geoarchaeological survey across the Rub' al Khali desert, researchers found no stone tools or knappable materials despite abundant freshwater at 55 of 125 sites. Spatial modeling indicates settlement required co-occurrence of water and lithic resources, with 57% of water-accessible terrain lacking suitable stone. The findings suggest that raw-material geology critically constrains early human dispersal, challenging models that prioritize water alone.",
  "key_findings": [
    "No lithic artefacts or knappable raw materials were found along a 1,500-k:km survey transect despite abundant freshwater",
    "57% of water-accessible terrain lacks knappable stone, indicating lithic resource constraints",
    "Spatial modeling shows settlement tracked co-occurrence of water and lithic resources, not water alone"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study demonstrates that lithic raw material availability, not water, was a critical constraint on early human dispersal across arid regions, revealing a previously overlooked geological factor in human migration models."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates iron's melting temperature and electrical resistivity under Earth core pressures using microsecond pulsed Joule heating in diamond anvil cells. Over 300 reproducible melting measurements reveal a shallow melting curve, reaching 6 K/GPa and  4160 ± 80 K at 200 GPa. The findings suggest a relatively cold core-mantle boundary (3430 K) and low adiabatic outer core heat flow (9.8 TW), indicating thermal convection alone could power the geodynamo before inner core formation and that the lowermost mantle remains fully solid.",
  "key_findings": [
    "A shallow melting curve for iron at 200 GPa, reaching 4160 ± 80 K",
    "Inferred core-mantle boundary temperature of 3430 K",
    "Low adiabatic outer core heat flow of 9.8 TW"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study provides critical constraints on iron's melting behavior and resistivity at high pressures, advancing understanding of Earth's core thermal evolution and geodynamo mechanisms."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how 4f electron filling influences bonding evolution in lanthanide single-atom catalysts across the lanthanide series. Using structurally uniform catalysts, researchers identified a dual-periodic trend in binding energies, increasing from La to Eu and Gd to Yb. The trend arises from electron redistribution between 5d and 4f orbitals, with a sharp transition at the half-filled 4f7 configuration (Eu to Gd). The findings demonstrate intrinsic electronic behavior across coordination environments, providing a framework for understanding lanthanide reactivity and catalyst design.",
  "key_findings": [
    "A dual-periodic trend in lanthanide-support binding energies increases from La to Eu and Gd to Yb",
    "Half-filled 4f7 configuration causes an abrupt transition between Eu and Gd",
    "Electron redistribution between 5d and 4f orbitals modulates bonding energies across diverse coordination environments"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study establishes a fundamental 4f-electron-regulated bonding framework that explains intrinsic reactivity patterns in lanthanides, advancing understanding of how electronic structure governs catalytic behavior across the series."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated regulatory T-cell dynamics in cirrhotic HCV genotype 4 patients during and after direct-acting antiviral (DAA) therapy. Using longitudinal flow cytometry, researchers compared 50 cirrhotic patients and 50 healthy controls at baseline, week 4, end of treatment, and 1 year post-SVR. Findings revealed baseline immune dysregulation with reduced T cells, profound CD8+ exhaustion, elevated activation markers, and expanded Tregs and TH17 cells. By 1 year post-SVR, exhausted CD8+ cells decreased by 66.2%, Tregs normalized, and TH1/TH2 subsets increased, indicating partial immune recovery after viral clearance.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Baseline cirrhotic patients showed reduced CD3+ T cells and profound CD8+ T-cell exhaustion (PD-1+ 71.0% vs. 11.1%)",
    "Tregs and TH17 cells were significantly elevated at baseline (52.7% vs. 15.1% and 25.8% vs. 14.0%)",
    "By 1 year post-SVR, exhausted PD-1+ CD8+ cells declined by 66.2%, Tregs normalized, and TH1/TH2 subsets increased"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study demonstrates that successful DAA therapy in cirrhotic HCV genotype 4 patients induces partial correction of peripheral T-cell dysregulation, providing insights into immune recovery mechanisms post-viral clearance in chronic liver disease."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates whether transient dietary changes at disease onset influence acute inflammatory responses and long-term tissue memory. Using lean mice with acute type 17 skin inflammation, researchers found that a 4-day high-fat diet (HFD) exposure during a specific peri-initiation window exacerbated disease severity to levels comparable with chronic obesity. HFD outside this window had no effect, while switching obese mice to a low-fat diet during the window reduced inflammation. Mechanistically, HFD rapidly recruited neutrophils, which expressed Nlrp3 and Il1b, driving pathogenic TH17 cell activation. Neutrophil depletion, Nlrp3 deficiency, or NLRP3 inhibition suppressed inflammation. Post-recovery, a chromatin-poised tissue-resident memory T cell population persisted, causing exaggerated inflammation upon recurrence despite LFD maintenance. The findings demonstrate that dietary state during a narrow temporal window can durably reprogram disease trajectory through neutrophil-NLRP3-IL-1β mechanisms.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Transient high-fat diet exposure during a 4-day peri-initiation window exacerbates acute type 17 skin inflammation in lean mice to chronic obesity levels.",
    "Switching obese mice to low-fat diet during the same window blunts disease amplification, indicating dietary intervention timing is critical.",
    "Neutrophil recruitment and NLRP3-IL-1β signaling drive pathogenic TH17 cell activation, with post-recovery tissue-resident memory T cells causing recurrent inflammation."
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes that short-lived dietary states during specific temporal windows can durably reprogram immune memory, revealing a neutrophil-NLRP3-IL-1β axis that licenses pathogenic TH17 responses."
}

{
  "summary": "This study quantifies the global carbon sinks using atmospheric oxygen and CO2 data from 1990 to 2019. It reveals a strengthening ocean carbon sink at 2.8 ± 0.4 Pg C year-1, accounting for 35% of fossil emissions, while the net land sink shows no significant trend (1.3 ± 0.5 Pg C year-1). The findings challenge existing model simulations, indicating potential inaccuracies in predicting future climate responses.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The ocean carbon sink strengthened by 2.8 ± 0.4 Pg C year-1 between 1990 and 2019",
    "The net land sink exhibited no robust trend despite large decadal variability",
    "The results contradict process-based model simulations of land and ocean sinks"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study provides critical empirical constraints on carbon sink dynamics, enhancing understanding of how natural systems absorb anthropogenic CO2 emissions and challenging model projections for climate mitigation strategies."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates enhancing capacitive deionization (CDI) for selective ammonium recovery from wastewater using molecularly engineered organic materials. Researchers designed 1,4-bis(1H-imidazo[4,5-b]phenazin-2-yl)benzene (IPB) to overcome the capacity-selectivity trade-off in CDI. The IPB-based CDI achieved high ammonium adsorption capacity (267.23 mg g⁻¹) and selectivity in complex streams, reducing ammonium to below 3 mg L⁻¹ and enabling 82.3% recovery as fertilizer. It also demonstrated low carbon emissions (4.66 × 10⁻² t CO₂-eq per tonne NH₄⁺ treated).",
  "key_findings": [
    "IPB-based CDI achieved 267.23 mg g⁻¹ ammonium adsorption capacity",
    "The system maintained high selectivity in complex wastewater matrices",
    "CDI reduced ammonium to below 3 mg L⁻¹ with 82.3% fertilizer recovery"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a molecularly engineered organic electrode material that enables high-capacity, selective ammonium capture from wastewater with low carbon footprint, advancing sustainable electrochemical water treatment technologies."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated whether reducing dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) concentration from 10% to 5% during cryopreservation preserves the biological integrity of GMP-compliant natural regulatory T (nTreg) products. Using integrated analyses, the researchers evaluated cryopreserved cells for viability, phenotypic purity, functional activity, epigenetic markers, and transcriptomic profiles. Results showed that reduced DMSO cryopreservation maintained key functional and molecular characteristics without significant adverse effects, supporting its clinical applicability for therapeutic Treg products.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Reduced DMSO cryopreservation (5% vs. 10%) preserved phenotypic purity and suppressive function of nTreg products after thawing",
    "Epigenetic lineage identity was maintained through FOXP3 Treg-specific demethylation region (TSDR) analysis",
    "No significant differences in transcriptomic profiles were observed between 5% and 10% DMSO conditions"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates that reduced-DMSO cryopreservation strategies can maintain critical functional and molecular characteristics of therapeutic nTreg products, advancing the feasibility of cryopreserved Treg therapies for clinical use."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the demographic processes behind the spread of Celtic languages during the Iron Age using ancient genomic data from 4,587 individuals across Bronze and Iron Age Europe. By analyzing genome-wide data, the researchers identified three key population movements: Steppe-related ancestry arriving via Bell Beaker groups around 4400 BP, a Bronze Age migration from southwestern Europe to Britain, and a westward expansion of eastern central European ancestry (3200-2800 BP) linked to Hallstatt and La Tène cultures. The findings suggest that Celtic language dispersal most plausibly followed the eastern central European migration, though the study emphasizes that genetic patterns do not always align with linguistic shifts.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Steppe-related ancestry entered Europe via Bell Beaker-associated groups around 4400 BP",
    "Bronze Age migration from southwestern Europe to Britain occurred between 3800-3200 BP",
    "Eastern central European ancestry expanded westward from 3200-2800 BP, influencing regions associated with Iron Age Celtic cultures"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides genomic evidence supporting a specific demographic pathway for Celtic language dispersal, clarifying the interplay between population movements and linguistic spread in prehistoric Europe."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated how stem-like CD4+ T cells differentiate in colorectal cancer (CRC) to influence early immune responses. Using imaging mass cytometry in CRC patients and mouse models, researchers observed TH1-like immune architectures in mismatch repair-deficient tumors linked to PD-1 immunotherapy response. In mismatch repair-proficient tumors, regulatory T cells dominated. PD-1 blockade induced stem-like CD4+ T cells to become TH1-like cells, which, when combined with OX4.0 agonist, suppressed tumor growth by expanding early effector CD4+ intermediates and promoting interferon-γ-dependent immune activation.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Mismatch repair-deficient CRC tumors exhibit TH1-like immune architectures associated with PD-1 immunotherapy response",
    "PD-1 blockade drives stem-like CD4+ T cells to differentiate into TH1-like cells",
    "Combined OX40 agonist and PD-1 blockade suppresses tumor progression by expanding Il7r+ early effector CD4+ intermediates"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The findings demonstrate that amplifying peripheral CD4+ T cell differentiation can enhance immunotherapy efficacy in colorectal cancer by promoting tumor-infiltrating TH1-like responses and associated immune activation."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates solar-driven ammonia synthesis using a 5 wt% Ru/Nb-CeO2 catalyst. By incorporating Nb5+ into CeO2, the catalyst induces compressive microstrain, suppressing oxygen sublattice reduction and maintaining Ru dispersion. In situ spectroscopy and computational analyses confirm an electron-enriched Ru state under reaction conditions. The catalyst achieves 7.4 mmol g-1 h-1 NH3 synthesis at 350°C with 144 h stability, demonstrating enhanced N2 activation and hydrogenation kinetics. The findings highlight lattice strain engineering as a viable strategy for robust, scalable solar ammonia production.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Nb5+ incorporation into CeO2 induces compressive microstrain, suppressing oxygen sublattice reduction",
    "In situ results confirm an electron-enriched Ru state under reaction conditions",
    "5 wt% Ru/Nb-CeO2 achieves 7.4 mmol g-1 h-1 NH3 synthesis with 144 h stability"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study establishes lattice strain engineering as a strategy to simultaneously stabilize oxygen sublattice and electronically promote Ru for efficient solar-driven ammonia synthesis."
}

{
  "summary": "This review investigates lactate's role in lung cancer by examining lactylation-mediated remodeling of spatial immunometabolic niches. It explores how lactate, produced under hypoxia and oncogenic signaling, acts as a carbon source, redox regulator, and precursor for lysine lactylation. The study identifies key lactylation modifications in histones and proteins that influence transcription, immune evasion, metabolic adaptation, and treatment resistance. Lactate also reprograms immune and stromal cells across tumor microenvironments, highlighting metabolic origins and cellular interactions in lung cancer progression.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Lactate serves as a carbon source and redox regulator in lung cancer cells",
    "Lactylation modifications (e.g., H3K18la, PD-L1) impact transcription, immune evasion, and treatment resistance",
    "Tumor-derived lactate reprograms CD8+ T cells, macrophages, and cancer-associated fibroblasts across spatial niches"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes lactate-driven lactylation as a critical mechanism linking metabolic reprogramming to immunometabolic crosstalk in lung cancer, providing a framework for targeted therapeutic interventions."
}

{
  "summary": "This study quantifies pre-cancerous evolutionary dynamics in acute myeloid leukaemia using serial blood samples collected up to 15 years before diagnosis. By deep duplex sequencing and evolutionary modeling, it identifies early driver mutations occurring in the first few decades of life within highly polyclonal blood. These slow-growing clones later acquire multiple driver mutations with up to 100-fold greater selective advantages, leading to somatic sweeps decades prior to diagnosis. The findings reveal simple evolutionary rules balancing stochastic and deterministic processes in pre-leukaemic evolution.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Early driver mutations arise in the first few decades of life within highly polyclonal blood",
    "Subsequent driver mutations confer up to 100-fold greater selective advantages than early drivers",
    "Clonal sweeps of blood occur decades before diagnosis, strongly linked to future acute myeloid leukaemia"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes simple evolutionary rules governing pre-cancerous dynamics in acute myeloid leukaemia, revealing how stochastic and deterministic processes balance to drive clonal expansion prior to malignancy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study examines political dynamics within the U.S. Congress, focusing on Democratic representation and legislative capacity. The analysis reveals that despite a majority Democratic presence, structural constraints significantly limit achievable policy outcomes. The research highlights persistent challenges in translating majority support into concrete legislative results, emphasizing systemic barriers to effective governance.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Democrats hold a majority but face structural constraints on legislative outcomes",
    "Majority representation does not guarantee policy achievements",
    "Systemic barriers impede the translation of political majority into legislative results"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study provides insight into the interplay between political representation and legislative efficacy, contributing to understanding how majority presence can be constrained by institutional structures in democratic governance."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates dynamic granular hydrogels with programmable microgel degradation for tissue repair. By engineering microgels with tunable hydrolytic susceptibility of hyaluronic acid polymers, the researchers created hydrogels that degrade over specific timeframes (days to weeks). In a mouse muscle injury model, slow-degrading hydrogels promoted organized extracellular matrix assembly, reduced fibrosis, enhanced muscle regeneration, and improved functional recovery compared to non-degrading or fast-degrading counterparts. The findings highlight the critical role of timed pore expansion in guiding tissue repair processes.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Slow-degrading hydrogels supported spatial organization of cell-instructive interstitial matrix and basement membrane proteins",
    "Slow-degrading hydrogels reduced fibrosis and enhanced myogenesis in muscle repair",
    "Slow-degrading hydrogels improved functional force recovery compared to non-degrading or fast-degrading hydrogels"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes that controlled microgel degradation kinetics in granular hydrogels can precisely guide extracellular matrix assembly and tissue regeneration, providing a tunable biomaterial strategy for dynamic wound healing processes."
}

{
  "summary": "This review examines regulatory T cell (Treg) therapy for heart transplantation, evaluating mechanistic evidence, clinical trials, and translational approaches. It compares peripheral-blood and thymus-derived Treg preparations, assesses their stability and monitoring in cardiac transplants, and identifies potential safety signals like amphiregulin and CXCL1. The study highlights challenges in ensuring long-term Treg persistence and functional stability post-transplantation.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Peripheral-blood and thymus-derived Treg cells differ in source, manufacturing, and potency for heart transplantation",
    "Amphiregulin and CXCL15-related signaling may serve as safety indicators rather than validated clinical biomarkers",
    "Human evidence for thymus-derived Tregs in heart transplantation includes limited case reports and preliminary data from early trials"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a translational framework for evaluating regulatory T cell therapy in cardiac transplantation, emphasizing persistent, stable, and monitorable Treg function as critical factors for clinical translation."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the molecular mechanism of replication fork restart via repriming by PRIMPOL in eukaryotic cells. Using real-time single-molecule imaging in nuclear extracts, the researchers visualized how stalled forks restart through PRIMPOL-mediated repriming. Key findings include PRIMPOL recruitment via CMG interaction, primer handover to polymerase ε, and a CMG-dependent primary repriming pathway that minimizes mutagenic synthesis without checkpoint activation. The study identifies a simple mechanism for targeted repriming and suggests potential inhibition strategies.",
  "key_findings": [
    "PRIMPOL is recruited to stalled replication forks through interaction with the CMG complex",
    "PRIMPOL facilitates primer handover to high-fidelity polymerase ε, minimizing mutagenic DNA synthesis",
    "A CMG-dependent primary repriming pathway operates without DNA damage checkpoint activation"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work elucidates a precise molecular mechanism for replication fork restart via repriming, providing insights into how cells maintain genomic integrity during replication stress without error-prone repair pathways."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates Earth's carbon sink capacity using observational data. It found that oceans absorb more carbon than previously estimated, while land sinks show reduced carbon uptake. The results indicate a shift in global carbon absorption dynamics, with implications for understanding climate change mechanisms and carbon cycle feedbacks.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Oceans absorb more carbon than previously estimated",
    "Land carbon sinks show reduced uptake",
    "Global carbon absorption dynamics have shifted"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The findings provide critical insights into recent changes in Earth's carbon sink capacity, enhancing understanding of how natural systems respond to climate change."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates enhancing cuproptosis efficacy by eliminating resistance mechanisms using lanthanide/transition metal ion-coordinated nanoparticles. The researchers designed La-Cu@Lap/IWR-1, which leverages La3+ to boost Cu2+-mediated oxidative stress and disrupt lysosomal acidification, while lapatinib and Wnt inhibitor IWR-1 counteract tumor resistance pathways. The approach significantly intensifies cuproptosis, triggering immunogenic cell death and inhibiting metastasis. The findings demonstrate a novel strategy for overcoming cuproptosis resistance in tumor therapy.",
  "key_findings": [
    "La3+ enhances Cu2+-mediated oxidative stress and disrupts lysosomal acidification",
    "Lapatinib and Wnt inhibitor IWR-1 synergistically suppress tumor resistance pathways",
    "The nanoparticle strategy induces immunogenic cell death and inhibits metastasis"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes lanthanide/transition metal coordination as a mechanism to overcome cuproptosis resistance, advancing targeted tumor therapy through dual modulation of oxidative stress and metastatic pathways."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated whether in vivo transduction of hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs) could safely produce CAR T cells targeting HER2high tumors. Using helper-dependent adenovirus vectors to express mHER2-CAR in mobilized mice, researchers demonstrated that continuously generated low-affinity CAR T cells significantly delayed tumor growth in immunocompetent models. Treg depletion further prolonged tumor-free periods. The approach showed minimal safety concerns but identified obstacles limiting efficacy that require future resolution.",
  "key_findings": [
    "In vivo transduction of HSCs produced mHER2-CAR T cells that delayed HER2high tumor growth in mice",
    "Treg depletion extended tumor-free periods by reducing relapse from mHER2-negative cells",
    "The approach maintained an excellent safety profile despite continuous CAR T cell production"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a safe in vivo strategy for generating tumor-targeting CAR T cells, providing a foundation for overcoming limitations in solid tumor immunotherapy through continuous, low-affinity T cell production."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates passive load regulation in tidal turbines using flexible rubber blades with chordwise trailing-edge deformation. The researchers experimentally compared flexible blades to rigid blades with active pitch control under simulated tidal cycles. Results show equivalent energy yield while reducing high-frequency fatigue loads by up to one-third through aeroelastic deformation that flattens blade camber. This passive approach offers improved durability without added mechanical complexity.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Flexible rubber blades achieve equivalent total energy yield compared to rigid blades with active pitch control",
    "High-frequency fatigue-inducing load fluctuations are mitigated by up to one-third via aeroelastic deformation",
    "Chordwise trailing-edge deformation flattens blade camber under load, enabling structural robustness"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "Passive load regulation through flexible blade design provides a cost-effective pathway for enhancing tidal turbine durability and performance without complex mechanical systems."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates enhancing tumor sensitivity to immunotherapy by engineering the immune cell environment surrounding cancers. The approach focuses on modulating the immune ecosystem to improve therapeutic responses. Results indicate that strategic manipulation of the immune milieu increases tumor vulnerability to immunotherapeutic interventions, suggesting potential for improved cancer treatment efficacy through immune-targeted strategies.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Strategic manipulation of the immune cell environment enhances tumor sensitivity to immunotherapy",
    "Engineering the immune ecosystem increases tumor vulnerability to immunotherapeutic interventions",
    "The approach improves therapeutic efficacy through immune-targeted strategies"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work advances understanding of how immune ecosystem engineering can overcome immunotherapy resistance mechanisms in cancer treatment."
}
